  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Living in southwest Ohio means paying for two very different seasons. From December through February, your heat runs against freezing nights and gray, damp days. By July, the air conditioner is fighting humidity that settles over the Great Miami River valley. Renters in West Carrollton cannot replace the furnace or add insulation to the attic, but there is still a lot you can control. Small, renter-friendly changes can noticeably lower your monthly bills and reduce your environmental footprint at the same time.</p>

<h2>Start by knowing what you actually pay for</h2>
<p>Before changing habits, look at which utilities are in your name. Some West Carrollton apartments include water, sewer, and trash in rent, while you pay electric and possibly natural gas directly. Others bundle a flat utility fee. Knowing the split tells you where your effort pays off. If your heat is electric, winter strategies matter most for your electric bill. If you have a gas furnace and gas water heater, those habits show up on a separate statement.</p>
<p>If you are still apartment hunting, ask each property what the average utility cost runs for the unit you are touring. It is one of the biggest <a href="/blog/hidden-costs-of-renting-an-apartment/">hidden costs of renting</a>, and it should go into your budget alongside rent. Our <a href="/tools/rent-affordability-calculator/">rent affordability calculator</a> can help you see how utilities change what you can comfortably spend.</p>

<h2>Winter: keep the heat you are paying for</h2>
<h3>Find and stop drafts</h3>
<p>On a windy January day, hold your hand near window edges, outlet covers on exterior walls, and the bottom of your entry door. If you feel cold air, you are losing heat. Removable fixes work well for renters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Window insulation film</strong> creates an extra air pocket over single-pane or leaky windows. It goes on with double-sided tape and a hair dryer, and comes off in spring.</li>
<li><strong>Draft stoppers</strong> slide under doors and block one of the biggest cold-air paths in older buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Thermal curtains</strong> closed at night add a layer of insulation. Open them on sunny winter days to let free solar heat in, especially on south-facing windows.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a window will not latch, a door seal is torn, or the heat is not keeping up, report it to your landlord in writing. Ohio Revised Code 5321.04 requires landlords to keep supplied heating systems in good working order. This is not legal advice, but documenting repair requests protects you.</p>

<h3>Use the thermostat strategically</h3>
<p>Lowering the thermostat a few degrees while you sleep or are at work is one of the easiest savings available. A programmable or smart thermostat makes it automatic, but ask your landlord before swapping one in. If you cannot change the thermostat, simply build the habit of adjusting it when you leave and before bed. Warm bedding, a throw blanket on the couch, and slippers make a cooler setting feel comfortable.</p>

<h3>Do not block the vents</h3>
<p>Furniture pushed against registers or baseboard heaters forces the system to run longer. Leave a few inches of clearance and keep vents dusted. Also, keep doors to rooms you use open so warm air circulates.</p>

<h2>Summer: cool smarter, not harder</h2>
<p>Ohio summers are as much about humidity as heat. A few habits make a real difference:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Close blinds and curtains on sunny windows</strong> during the afternoon. West-facing units soak up heat late in the day.</li>
<li><strong>Raise the thermostat a few degrees</strong> when you are out. Coming home to a slightly warmer apartment costs far less than holding it cold all day.</li>
<li><strong>Use ceiling or box fans</strong> to feel cooler at a higher setting. Fans cool people, not rooms, so turn them off when you leave.</li>
<li><strong>Skip the oven on hot days.</strong> Use a slow cooker, air fryer, microwave, or grill (if your lease allows) to avoid adding heat indoors.</li>
<li><strong>Change or ask about the HVAC filter.</strong> A clogged filter makes the system work harder. Many leases put filter changes on the landlord, but some ask tenants to swap them. Check yours.</li>
</ul>

<!--products-->

<h2>Year-round electricity savings</h2>
<h3>Lighting and electronics</h3>
<p>Swap any remaining incandescent bulbs for LEDs. Keep the old bulbs in a box so you can put them back when you move out if your lease requires it. Electronics that sit in standby mode, like TVs, game consoles, soundbars, and printers, draw power around the clock. Plug them into a power strip or smart plug that you switch off at night.</p>

<h3>Laundry and dishes</h3>
<p>Wash clothes in cold water whenever possible, since heating water is a large share of laundry energy. Run full loads in both the washer and dishwasher. If you have an in-unit dryer, clean the lint filter every load and consider air-drying some items on a folding rack.</p>

<h3>Refrigerator basics</h3>
<p>Keep the fridge between roughly 37 and 40 degrees and the freezer near 0. Do not stand with the door open deciding what to eat, and let leftovers cool a bit before putting them away.</p>

<h2>Water: easy wins even if it is included</h2>
<p>Even if water is bundled into your rent, hot water still costs you through your gas or electric bill. Shorter showers, a low-flow shower head (keep the original), and reporting leaky faucets or running toilets quickly all help. A toilet that runs constantly can waste a surprising amount of water, and fixing it is the landlord's job in most leases.</p>

<h2>Sustainable habits that also save money</h2>
<p>Lowering your footprint in an apartment is mostly about consuming less. A few habits that pay off both ways:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Recycle correctly.</strong> Check your building's recycling setup and what Montgomery County accepts so your bins are not contaminated.</li>
<li><strong>Buy less, buy used.</strong> Secondhand furniture and decor keep items out of landfills. See our guide to <a href="/blog/furnish-apartment-on-a-budget/">furnishing an apartment on a budget</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Cut food waste.</strong> Plan meals, use clear containers so leftovers are visible, and freeze bread and produce before they spoil.</li>
<li><strong>Use reusable basics.</strong> Cloth towels, refillable water bottles, and reusable bags reduce both trash and repeat purchases.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Shop your energy supplier carefully</h2>
<p>If your electric or gas account is in your name, Ohio's energy choice program may let you choose a different supplier for the generation or commodity portion of your bill. The Public Utilities Commission of Ohio maintains comparison information. Look closely at whether a rate is fixed or variable, how long the term lasts, and whether there is an early termination fee. A low introductory rate that jumps after a few months is not a deal.</p>

<h2>Pick an efficient apartment from the start</h2>
<p>The cheapest kilowatt is the one you never use. When touring, notice window quality, whether the unit is a middle unit (shared walls help with heating and cooling), and the age of appliances. Our article on the best floor to live on covers how top-floor and ground-floor units differ in temperature. When you are ready to compare options, browse <a href="/apartments/">West Carrollton apartments</a> and ask each property about average utility costs.</p>
